Two Sum – Pair Count (C++)

Problem Statement

Given an array of integers and a target value, count the number of unique index pairs whose sum equals the target.

Example

Input:

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

// Counts unique index pairs whose sum equals target
int countPairs(vector<int>& arr, int target) {
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j < arr.size(); j++) {

            if (arr[i] + arr[j] == target) {
                count++;
            }
        }
    }

    return count;
}

int main() {
    vector<int> arr = {2, 4, 3, 5, 2};
    int target = 6;

    cout << "Total Pairs: "
         << countPairs(arr, target) << endl;

    return 0;
}

arr = {2, 4, 3, 5, 2}
target = 6

Output:

Total Pairs: 3

Valid Pairs:

- (2, 4)
- (4, 2) — using the second occurrence of 2
- (4, 2) — using the last occurrence of 2

Each pair represents different indices, not just different values.

Approach

- Use two nested loops.
- The outer loop selects the first element.
- The inner loop starts from "i + 1" to avoid using the same index twice.
- If the sum equals the target, increment the counter.

Debugging Insight

Original issue:

for (int j = i; j < arr.size(); j++)

Starting from "i" allows the same array index to be used twice.

Fixed version:

for (int j = i + 1; j < arr.size(); j++)

This ensures every pair uses two different indices and avoids counting the same pair in reverse order.

Complexity Analysis

Complexity| Value
Time| O(n²)
Auxiliary Space| O(1)

